\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Integers are supplied in binary; polynomial time means a polynomial number of classical bit operations in total input length. Define $\omega(n)=|\{p:p\text{ is prime and }p\mid n\}|$ for $n\ge1$, with $\omega(1)=0$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is there a deterministic polynomial-time algorithm which outputs $\omega(n)$ for every positive integer $n$? \href{https://mccurley.org/papers/open.ps.gz}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can the number of distinct prime divisors be computed deterministically in time polynomial in the binary input length?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 8400010 (AMR-083-0010). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Leonard M. Adleman and Kevin S. McCurley, Open Problems in Number Theory Complexity, II, in Algorithmic Number Theory, LNCS 877 (1994), pp. 291--322, C9/O9, manuscript p. 9 (LNCS p. 299). \url{https://mccurley.org/papers/open.ps.gz}.
\end{description}
